🔹 What is Git?

Git is a distributed version control system (DVCS) used to track changes in source code.

It was created in 2005 by Linus Torvalds (the creator of Linux).

🔹 Why Git Was Created?

Before Git, developers faced problems:

Code was overwritten

No proper version history

Difficult team collaboration

Hard to manage large projects

Git solved these problems by:

✔ Tracking every change
✔ Saving version history
✔ Supporting team collaboration
✔ Allowing branching and merging

🔹 What is Version Control?

Version control means:

Managing and tracking different versions of a file or project.

Example:

Version 1 → Add login page
Version 2 → Fix bug
Version 3 → Add new feature

Git saves all these versions safely.

🔹 Important Features of Git
1️⃣ Distributed System

Every developer has a full copy of the project.

2️⃣ Fast Performance

Git is very fast compared to older systems.

3️⃣ Branching

You can create branches to work on new features.

4️⃣ Merging

Combine different branches.

5️⃣ History Tracking

See who changed what and when.

🔹 Basic Git Workflow

Create project → git init

Add files → git add

Save version → git commit

Send to server → git push

Get updates → git pull

🔹 What is GitHub?

GitHub is a web-based platform that hosts Git repositories online.

It was launched in 2008 and later acquired by Microsoft in 2018.

🔹 Why GitHub is Used?

Git works locally.
GitHub works online.

GitHub allows:

✔ Store code in cloud
✔ Share code with others
✔ Team collaboration
✔ Pull requests
✔ Issue tracking
✔ CI/CD integration
